/** Retrofit batch AU -- upgrading old 2-case (and, for two problems, 0-case) gpe-history problems to
 * the 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress). All solvers below were independently
 * written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's real official Sample (or, where no real
 * Sample row exists, against the already-seeded TestCase) before being trusted to generate new
 * tricky/stress expected output.
 *
 * gpe-10645-euclid-problem: extended Euclid to get one particular solution (x0,y0), then the general
 *   solution family X=x0+t*(B/g), Y=y0-t*(A/g) is searched over a handful of integer t candidates near
 *   the two "one coordinate crosses zero" breakpoints to find the minimal |X|+|Y|, with ties broken by
 *   X<=Y (then Y ascending). Verified against brute force for many small (A,B) pairs.
 * gpe-10655-sumsets: classic largest-d-first search (d = a+b+c, all four distinct elements of S) via
 *   two-pointer scan over the FULL sorted array excluding d's own index -- NOT restricted to elements
 *   below d's position, since a valid triple can include elements larger than d if others are very
 *   negative (this was an actual bug caught while verifying against the already-seeded boundary case's
 *   negative-number dataset).
 * gpe-10658-is-bigger-smarter: sort elephants by weight ascending (ties broken by IQ ascending, which
 *   forces same-weight elephants apart in any strictly-decreasing-IQ subsequence), then an O(n^2)
 *   longest-strictly-decreasing-subsequence DP. The problem allows multiple valid maximum-length
 *   answers ("any one is acceptable"); this specific DP tie-breaking (first-found predecessor, LAST
 *   index among tied maximum lengths as the chain's start) was reverse-engineered to reproduce the
 *   real official Sample's exact output byte-for-byte. Because the checker is a strict text comparator
 *   and the problem is inherently multi-answer, a technically-correct alternative arrangement from a
 *   real submission could still be marked wrong -- this is a pre-existing checker-architecture
 *   limitation (would need a special judge), not something fixable via test data alone.
 * gpe-10675-urn-ball-probabilities: at round k, P(both red) = 1/(k*(k+1)) (urn1 has k balls incl. 1
 *   red, urn2 has k+1 balls incl. 1 red). P(at least one all-red pickup) = 1 - product_{k=1}^{N}(1 -
 *   1/(k(k+1))); P(all N pickups both red) = 1/(N!*(N+1)!), whose leading-zero count after the decimal
 *   point is floor(-log10(P)). Both computed via prefix-sum cumulative logs (natural log for the first
 *   quantity, log10 of factorials for the second) to stay fast and numerically stable up to N=999999.
 * gpe-10679-irreducible-basic-fractions: Euler's totient via O(sqrt(n)) trial-division factorization.
 * gpe-10732-snow-clearing: this reduces to a directed Chinese-Postman/Euler-circuit tour -- doubling
 *   every street into both directed lanes always yields an Eulerian circuit (in-degree=out-degree
 *   everywhere), so EVERY lane is plowed exactly once at the slow 20km/h rate and the faster
 *   already-plowed speed is never actually used; total time = 2*sum(street lengths)/20000, rounded to
 *   the nearest minute. Verified exactly against both real sample datasets.
 * gpe-10741-the-grand-dinner: modeled as max-flow (source->team_i cap m_i, team_i->table_j cap 1 <--
 *   enforces "at most one member of a team per table", table_j->sink cap n_j); feasible iff maxflow
 *   equals total members, and the capacity-1 team-table edges directly reveal a valid assignment.
 *   Verified exactly against both real sample datasets (including the infeasible second case).
 */
